<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roxana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 340–c. 310 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Roxane</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Roxana was a Bactrian noblewoman, daughter of Oxyartes, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> married in 327 BCE. She bore his posthumous son, Alexander IV, in 323 BCE, and mother and son became pawns in the wars of Alexander's successors. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a> held them captive at Amphipolis and had both killed around 310 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roxana.jpg" alt="Roxana" width="320" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pietro Rotari · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_The_Greate_and_Roxane_by_Rotari_1756.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Roxana was the daughter of the Bactrian nobleman Oxyartes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> and Sogdia, who served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessus">Bessus</a> and was probably involved in the murder of the last Achaemenid king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius III</a>. The date of her birth is unknown, but she was of childbearing age by 326 BCE. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> captured Bessus, Oxyartes and his family fled north and, with the Sogdian warlord Spitamenes, took up position in a fortress known as the Sogdian Rock, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonians</a> defeated them.</p><p>Alexander then reportedly fell in love with Roxana on sight at a celebration. Where it took place is disputed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a> states that Oxyartes surrendered when he learned how well Alexander had received his daughter, and A. B. Bosworth raises the possibility that she was captured at the Sogdian Rock and the two married at the fortress of Chorienes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The marriage took place in 327 BCE and, according to most sources, followed Macedonian rather than Persian custom. Alexander married her despite opposition from his companions, who would have preferred a Macedonian or Greek queen, and the match may also have been political, since the Bactrian and Sogdian armies became more loyal afterwards. According to the Metz Epitome she went with him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where their first son died in infancy near the Hydaspes in the autumn of 326 BCE. In 324 BCE Alexander also married Stateira II, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius III</a>.</p><p>After Alexander died suddenly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in 323 BCE, Roxana is believed to have murdered Stateira, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> says she had Stateira's sister Drypetis killed with the consent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perdiccas">Perdiccas</a>. She was pregnant, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonians</a> reached a compromise: Philip Arrhidaeus was declared king, and the unborn child was to be king as well if a son. She bore a son, Alexander IV, in 323 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>By 317 BCE the young Alexander had lost his kingship through the intrigues of Philip Arrhidaeus' wife, Eurydice II, and Roxana and her son were protected by Alexander's mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympias</a> in Macedonia. After Olympias was assassinated in 316 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a> imprisoned them in the citadel of Amphipolis. A peace treaty of 311 BCE confirmed Alexander IV's kingship with Cassander as his guardian, but Cassander ordered Glaucias of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> to kill the boy and Roxana, possibly by poison. Scholars disagree on the date of their deaths. Inscriptions at the Acropolis record offerings Roxana dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> as Alexander's wife, and the asteroid 317 Roxane is named for her.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Roxana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Sogdian noble Spitamenes destroyed a Macedonian force on the Polytimetus and fought on until his death in 328 BCE; in 327 BCE Alexander stormed the mountain fortress known as the Sogdian Rock and married <b>Roxana</b>, daughter of the Bactrian noble Oxyartes, who had been captured there.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sogdians">Sogdians · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 310 BCE he had Alexander IV and his mother <b>Roxane</b> killed, and in 305 BCE took the title of king.</blockquote><a href="/state/antipatrid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antipatrid Kingdom of Macedonia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Died in Amphipolis, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.76,66.9&amp;year=-321" aria-label="Bactria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6229 839 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Roxana"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6679 1109h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6679 1109h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.76,66.9&amp;year=-321">What was here in 321 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
